PART 1 — LOGIN TO AWS
Step 1: Open AWS Console
Open browser
Go to:

AWS Console

Click:
Sign In to Console
Enter:
Email
Password
PART 2 — CREATE FIRST EC2 INSTANCE (EFS-1)
Step 2: Open EC2 Dashboard

After login:

At top search bar type:
EC2
Click:
Amazon EC2
Step 3: Launch Instance

Inside EC2 dashboard:

Left side menu → click:
Instances
Top right → click orange button:
Launch instances
PART 3 — CONFIGURE EFS-1 SERVER
Step 4: Name Your Server

You will see:

"Name and tags"

Inside Name box type:

EFS-1
Step 5: Choose Operating System

Scroll slightly down.

Under:

Application and OS Images

Choose:

Amazon Linux

(Usually already selected)

Step 6: Select Instance Type

Find:

Instance type

Select:

t2.micro

This is free tier eligible.

Step 7: Create Key Pair

This is VERY IMPORTANT.

Find:

Key pair (login)

Click:

Create new key pair

A popup opens.

Enter:
Key pair name:
EFS
Choose:
Key pair type:
RSA
Private key format:
.pem

Click:

Create key pair

File downloads automatically.

⚠️ IMPORTANT:
Save this .pem file carefully.

PART 4 — NETWORK SETTINGS
Step 8: Configure Network

Find:

Network settings

Click:

Edit
Step 9: Create Security Group

Choose:

Create security group
Security Group Name:
EFS-SG
Step 10: Add NFS Rule

Scroll to:

Inbound security group rules

Click:

Add security group rule
New Rule:
Type	Source
NFS	Anywhere

NFS automatically uses:

Port 2049

This is required for EFS.

Step 11: Launch Instance

Bottom right click:

Launch instance

Wait 1–2 minutes.

You created:

EFS-1
PART 5 — CREATE SECOND INSTANCE (EFS-2)

Repeat same steps again.

Only change:

Name:
EFS-2

Everything else same.

Launch second instance.

PART 6 — CREATE EFS STORAGE
Step 12: Open EFS Service

Top AWS search bar:

EFS

Click:

Amazon Elastic File System
Step 13: Create File System

Click orange button:

Create file system
Step 14: Configure EFS
Name:
week_3_efs
VPC:

Choose:

default
File system type:

Choose:

Regional

Then click:

Customize

or

Next

(depending on AWS UI)

PART 7 — CONFIGURE EFS NETWORK
Step 15: Configure Mount Targets

You will see:

Availability Zones
Security groups
IMPORTANT:

Delete default security groups.

Then select:

EFS-SG

(the same security group created earlier)

Click:

Next

Then:

Create file system

Done.

PART 8 — CONNECT TO EC2 INSTANCES

Now we connect to servers.

Step 16: Open EC2 Instances Page

Go back to:

EC2 Dashboard
Instances

You will see:

EFS-1
EFS-2
Step 17: Copy Public IP

Click:

EFS-1

Find:

Public IPv4 address

Copy it.

Do same for:

EFS-2
PART 9 — CONNECT USING POWERSHELL / TERMINAL
For Windows Users

Open:

PowerShell
Step 18: Move to PEM File Folder

Example:

cd Downloads
Step 19: Connect to EFS-1

Type:

ssh -i EFS.pem ec2-user@YOUR-IP

Example:

ssh -i EFS.pem ec2-user@13.233.xx.xx

If asked:

Are you sure?

Type:

yes
PART 10 — INSTALL EFS UTILITIES
Step 20: Become Root User

Type:

sudo su
Step 21: Create Folder

Type:

mkdir efs
Step 22: Install EFS Package

Type:

yum install -y amazon-efs-utils

Wait until installation completes.

Step 23: Repeat for EFS-2

Open another PowerShell window.

Repeat:

SSH login
sudo su
mkdir efs
install package

for:

EFS-2
PART 11 — ATTACH EFS TO EC2
Step 24: Open EFS File System

Go to:

EFS dashboard
Click your EFS
Step 25: Click Attach

Top right click:

Attach

Popup opens.

Step 26: Select Mount via DNS

Choose:

Mount via DNS

You will see a command like:

sudo mount -t efs fs-xxxxx:/ efs

Copy it.

Step 27: Run Command on BOTH Instances

Paste command in:

EFS-1 terminal
EFS-2 terminal

Press Enter.

Now EFS storage is attached.

PART 12 — TEST FILE SHARING
Step 28: Go Into EFS Folder

On BOTH terminals type:

cd efs
Step 29: Create File in EFS-1

On EFS-1 type:

touch hello.txt

Check:

ls

You will see:

hello.txt
Step 30: Verify in EFS-2

On EFS-2 type:

ls

You should ALSO see:

hello.txt

🎉 SUCCESS!

Both EC2 servers are sharing same storage.
